Instead of saving lots of copies manually, Git remembers every change you make.

Whenever i finish some work i will tell to git save my work

"Remember the version"

Later if something i need to modify or restore the deleted work , Git can bring back any previous verisons with in seconds.

**Think of Git like CCTV footage:**

Imagine a shop has CCTV cameras.

If something happens today, you can watch yesterday's recording.

You don't need to remember what happened.

The camera recorded everything.

Git works the same way.

Instead of recording videos...

It records changes in your code.

Yesterday
    ↓
Today
    ↓
Tomorrow

Git lets you go back to any point.

**Why do software companies use Git?**

Imagine 10 developers are working on the same project.

Without Git...

Person A changes Login page.

Person B changes Login page.

Person C changes Login page.

Now everyone's files are different.

Nobody knows whose changes are correct.

Chaos.

But With Git...

Everyone works independently.

Git combines their changes.

If two people edit the same line, Git points it out so they can resolve the conflict.

**Where does Git store everything?**

Inside your project folder, Git creates a hidden folder called:

```python
.git
```

Think of it as a secret diary.

It stores:

Every saved version
Who made each change
When it was made
What changed
Why it changed (write the commit message)

Your project might look like:

ShoppingApp/

    .git/
    Login.java
    Payment.java
    Home.java

Users only see your project files.

Git quietly keeps track of all the history in the hidden .git folder.

